{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map","id":1383948,"title":"Pantheon Map","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-04-26T06:22:43-07:00","id":1383948,"title":"Pantheon Map","content":"I constructed a world-map to display the locations of as many of the pantheons as possible. It currently has the locations of 42 of them. I will update it in the future, and feel free to point out any errors. <a href=\"https://www.mapcustomizer.com/map/Map%20of%20World%20Pantheons\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.mapcustomizer.com/map/Ma...ld%20Pantheons</a><br /> <br /> Edit: It's updated! With 22 more pantheons!<br /> <br /> Edit 2: Made a few corrections in regards to terminology, and added the Pawnee pantheon, which I'm tentatively referring to as the Nahurac. I'll probably be picking at this for a while. 65 pantheons total!<br /> <br /> Edit 3: 21 more! 86 in total! Most of the new ones are pantheons I know very little about personally, but which I'm aware are options.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","user":"Wannabe Demon Lord","date":". 04-27-2020, 09:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-04-27T06:19:06-07:00","id":1384175,"title":"","content":"If anyone knows the names of the Guanche, Bambara, Chumash, Oromo, Purepecha, Efik, Wabanaki, Cahuilla, Maasai, Salish, Dinka, Wichita, Aonikenk, or Sinhalese pantheons, they would be greatly appreciated!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-03T05:48:48-07:00","id":1385393,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map#post1383948\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">added the Pawnee pantheon</div> </div> </div> </div>Praise be to Swanson. Salutations to the Mother-Of-All, Knope <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> In all seriousness this is a really cool tool, thanks!<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26056-cassiecataria","name":"CassieCataria","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26056_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-03T09:45:39-07:00","id":1385422,"title":"","content":"No problem! The Pawnee religion is actually really interesting. There's some human sacrifice involved. Unfortunately, information is pretty scarce, so I'm having trouble determining whether the gods were doing it out of pragmatism like the Teotl or were just really gruesome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-03T11:27:55-07:00","id":1385434,"title":"","content":"This is really cool! I liked how I would think of one that I wasn't sure was on here and then found it - this is supercomprehensive<br /> <br /> Out of curiosity, does anyone know of a map like this, but where it shows important places from mythology, or the places that they supposedly correspond to? (I.e. Mount Olympus, various underworld crossing points, etc)?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1407-endlesskng","name":"EndlessKng","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-07T01:05:50-07:00","id":1386310,"title":"","content":"Well this is cool! It's interesting to see the diversity and distribution of pantheons around the world - especially how some regions are relatively crowded while others are almost empty. I guess that says something about Western scholarship and about which cultures we know (or think of) most/least. There's also a lot of new pantheons to discover that I've never heard about, so this is quite inspiring!<br /> <br /> If you're interested in some more suggestions, there's a few pantheons I'm aware of that are still missing. Mind you, I know very little about most of these, so take them with a grain of salt.<br /> - The Ayyaana (Oromo and possibly Somali people, Eastern Africa)<br /> - The Benzhu (Bai people, mostly of Yunnan Province, China)<br /> - The Hanitu (Bunun people of Taiwan)<br /> - The Jinzami (Bakongo, modern day DR Congo &amp; Angola)<br /> - The Pangool (Serer people. modern Senegal/Gambia)<br /> <br /> And some more complicated ones:<br /> - The Orenda (the core book presents the Haudenosaunee/Iroquois pantheon as part of the Manitou, although I have some doubts if they shouldn't be related but separate pantheon)<br /> - The Malakhim (the angels of the Abrahamic religions; I know they are quite contentious around the forum, but if you want to include them on the map, I believe locating them in Jerusalem <i>might</i> be acceptable to all three major Abrahamic faiths)<br /> - The Aeons (emanations of God in various Gnostic beliefs)<br /> - The Ascended Masters (of Theosophy)<br /> - The Kingdom of Fanes (a royal family from Ladin literature that at least some people believe may have once been gods; located in the Dolomites of South Tyrol, Italy)<br /> <br /> There's also a few cultures that I believe have (or in some cases had) their own pantheons that I don't have names for right now. Including the Dravidians, the Fulani, the Hungarians, the Kirati, the Meitei, the Tani, the Yi, the Zapotecs and the Zhuang.<br /> <br /> Finally, I have some nitpicky suggestions for edits that I noticed:<br /> - The Viet pantheon are called the Than, not the Thanh. It's a bit confusing because 'Th\u1ea7n' means 'god', while 'Th\u00e1nh' means 'saint'.<br /> - I believe the Hausa spirits are called the Iskoki, not Bori (which I believe is the name for their concept of spirit, or something)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Pantheon:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1d6MGe0uK0nLsOABBu3YymfU-7gS7Nwur/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Th\u1ea7n of Vietnam</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25842-mr-wopsle","name":"Mr Wopsle","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25842_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-07T17:09:36-07:00","id":1386558,"title":"","content":"Thank you so much for the kind words and the feedback! I've incorporated most of those. And don't be afraid to nitpick. There's honestly a number of pantheon names on there that I'm really unsure about. Most of the pantheons you named are ones I didn't know about, and which I'd love to find out more on. I'd be happy to look at any sources you do happen to have on them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13079-wannabe-demon-lord","name":"Wannabe Demon Lord","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-28T15:40:34-08:00","id":1526085,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wannabe Demon Lord</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map?p=1384175#post1384175\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If anyone knows the names of the Guanche, Bambara, Chumash, Oromo, Purepecha, Efik, Wabanaki, Cahuilla, Maasai, Salish, Dinka, Wichita, Aonikenk, or Sinhalese pantheons, they would be greatly appreciated!</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The Wichita consider their gods &quot;dreams&quot;, it is something similar to the concept of Manitou. It would be The Itska<br /> We may never find Aonikenk, the language is extinct and there is little information about it.<br /> BUT, they are part of the Telhuelche family, also extinct, but much more studied and among the terms collected there is the name of the pantheon, The Otil Nau &quot;Good Spirits&quot;<br /> <br /> Of the Sinhalese pantheon, perhaps we will not find it, Sri Lanka has been Buddhist for most of its history, the only thing known about the native religion are ancient sightings that they worshiped snakes.<br /> The closest are the Vedda People who live there, it is disputed whether they are descendants of the indigenous inhabitants of Sri Lanka.<br /> In any case, they worship The N\u00e4 Yakku, an animist cult.<br /> <br /> \u200b<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28200-plebsparks","name":"PlebSparks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28200_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-29T15:20:57-08:00","id":1526137,"title":"","content":"When Buddhism spread to new countries, it often incorporated the existing pantheon in as protectors of the buddhadharma (Buddhism). S.J. Tambiah's <i>Buddhism and the Spirit Cults</i> in North-East Thailand extensively discusses the relationship between Buddhism and the Buddhist monastic order and the indigenous animism of Thailand and their priests. There are fan pantheon write-ups of the native animist pantheons of Buddhist Burma and Vietnam. I suppose it's possible the native Sinhalese pantheon was completely erased but I wouldn't be surprised to learn they survive in a similar form.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26114-muskrat","name":"Muskrat","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-29T18:00:52-08:00","id":1526145,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Muskrat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map?p=1526137#post1526137\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When Buddhism spread to new countries, it often incorporated the existing pantheon in as protectors of the buddhadharma (Buddhism). S.J. Tambiah's <i>Buddhism and the Spirit Cults</i> in North-East Thailand extensively discusses the relationship between Buddhism and the Buddhist monastic order and the indigenous animism of Thailand and their priests. There are fan pantheon write-ups of the native animist pantheons of Buddhist Burma and Vietnam. I suppose it's possible the native Sinhalese pantheon was completely erased but I wouldn't be surprised to learn they survive in a similar form.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is an animist pantheon, King Pandukabhaya, grandfather of Devaanampiya Tissa, even dedicated shrines to these indigenous deities, which external influences would later designate as Yaksas and Yaksinis.<br /> Among those that have names: Kaalavela, Cittaraaja, Vessavana, Valavaamukhi and Cittaa. Vyaadhadeva, Kammaaradeva and Pacchimaraajini<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://web.facebook.com/525936864410505/posts/buddhism-in-sri-lanka-part-2see-distribution5-pre-buddhist-religion-in-sri-lanka/1155479074789611/\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://scontent-atl3-1.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t1.6435-9/100564762_1155479034789615_6631126306102706176_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s552x414&amp;_nc_cat=110&amp;ccb=1-7&amp;_nc_sid=512d91&amp;_nc_ohc=DeUzo9zIWHMAX-KdilT&amp;_nc_ht=scontent-atl3-1.xx&amp;oh=00_AfB803puCLHPt0pqj6_DmIvqGcJVx8HcnNlm0BSJNyfkiQ&amp;oe=658F5988\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://web.facebook.com/525936864410505/posts/buddhism-in-sri-lanka-part-2see-distribution5-pre-buddhist-religion-in-sri-lanka/1155479074789611/\" target=\"_blank\"> \u0dc3\u0dd2\u0dad \u0db1\u0dd2\u0dc0\u0dd9\u0db1 \u0db6\u0dab \u0d9a\u0dad\u0dcf </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://web.facebook.com/525936864410505/posts/buddhism-in-sri-lanka-part-2see-distribution5-pre-buddhist-religion-in-sri-lanka/1155479074789611/\" target=\"_blank\"> https://web.facebook.com/525936864410505/posts/buddhism-in-sri-lanka-part-2see-distribution5-pre-buddhist-religion-in-sri-lanka/1155479074789611/ </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Buddhism in Sri Lanka - Part 2 See distribution 5. Pre-Buddhist Religion in Sri Lanka It is evident from the chronicles relating the early history of Sri Lanka that before the introduction of... </div> </div> </div><br /> They are not as analyzed as the Burmese or Tibetan gods, and I don't think calling them Yaksas directly is a good idea, Devi could work, but I'm not sure if it is the indigenous word or if it is influenced by &quot;Deva&quot; from hindi.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28200,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28200-plebsparks","user":"PlebSparks","date":". 11-29-2023, 09:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28200-plebsparks","name":"PlebSparks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28200_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-09T04:17:28-08:00","id":1528255,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Arial\">If I may suggest some changes:<br /> <br /> Guarani Pantheon should be closer to the Paraguai/Brazilian border (although there are Guarani in a lot of places, Paraguai has Guarani as one of their official languages, and I know they have a relatively large population there).<br /> The Kadiweu pantheon in Mato Grosso (The Ohoenrodite)<br /> The Tukano/Desana in the Amazon Region, near the Rio Negro region (The \u00d5\u2019\u00c2mar\u00e3)<br /> </span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","name":"Manbat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12934_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-10T22:57:17-08:00","id":1528352,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Manbat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map?p=1528255#post1528255\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">If I may suggest some changes:<br /> <br /> Guarani Pantheon should be closer to the Paraguai/Brazilian border (although there are Guarani in a lot of places, Paraguai has Guarani as one of their official languages, and I know they have a relatively large population there).<br /> The Kadiweu pantheon in Mato Grosso (The Ohoenrodite)<br /> The Tukano/Desana in the Amazon Region, near the Rio Negro region (The \u00d5\u2019\u00c2mar\u00e3)<br /> </span></div> </div> </div> </div>What do those pantheon names mean in their native language?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28200-plebsparks","name":"PlebSparks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28200_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-13T08:25:44-08:00","id":1528484,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The \u00d5\u2019\u00c2mar\u00e3</span> means &quot;gods&quot; in one of the Tukano dialects while Ohoenrodite means &quot;mythological heroes&quot; in Kadiweu. I don't have a name for the Guarani pantheon right now, but that is certainly something I will look upon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","name":"Manbat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12934_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-13T12:10:40-08:00","id":1528492,"title":"","content":"Another of the fixes and updates that I have seen that the map should have is with some names.<br /> Ossetian - Dzuar (Uac is a less precise abbreviation for saint)<br /> Ashaninka - Tasorenci<br /> Mixtec - Nuhu (not -uhu)<br /> Zulu - Tixo (Unkulunkulu is not a theonym, it means &quot;the great of greats&quot; and would only apply to the main deity)<br /> Dogon - N/A (Nommo would not apply for deity, the descriptions make it seem more like a type of ancient creature than anything else)<br /> Miskito - Lasa<br /> Huichol - Tutuma<br /> Yakut - Aiyy<br /> Warao - Jebu<br /> Innu - Manitu<br /> Lenape \u2013 Mantu/Pa'tumawas (an alternative word to differentiate it from Innu pantheon)<br /> Solomon Islands - Hi'ona<br /> Although I have a question, how do we do this for the Polynesian pantheons.<br /> I have 3 completely independent pantheons (Samoa, Bellona-Renell and Rapa Nui) but they are ALL called Atua, so without variants<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Manbat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map?p=1528484#post1528484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The \u00d5\u2019\u00c2mar\u00e3</span> means &quot;gods&quot; in one of the Tukano dialects while Ohoenrodite means &quot;mythological heroes&quot; in Kadiweu. I don't have a name for the Guarani pantheon right now, but that is certainly something I will look upon.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Wasn't Yibi\u00e1s the name of the Guarani pantheon?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28200,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28200-plebsparks","user":"PlebSparks","date":". 01-13-2024, 03:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28200-plebsparks","name":"PlebSparks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28200_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-13T13:31:58-08:00","id":1528494,"title":"","content":"The Yibi\u00e1s were a Tupi-Guarani pantheon, so they had gods of the Tupi and Guarani, which are two cultural lineages that are close together and share a bunch of myths, names, and so on. The Yibi\u00e1s is a Tupi name (and means &quot;Those who zeal for the World&quot;), and since I produced Pindorama I have wondered if they wouldn't be better served being split apart. I still hold that they can fit together well as a somewhat concise cultural branch (or as concise as a disparate but related group of communities can be), but I have been tinkering about making a more Guarani distinction of them. If you want to keep them together as a single marking on the map you can put them as Yibi\u00e1s (and more on a Central-South position in Brazil), but otherwise, you can keep them separate.<br /> <br /> You can also mark the Tupi gods as Yibi\u00e1s (closer to the Brazilian Coast), but a purely Tupi pantheon is... complicated, as the sources are few and disparate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","name":"Manbat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12934_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-13T18:52:11-08:00","id":1528511,"title":"","content":"The Mediterranean basin would be complex. Rome spread the gods around. Britain alone had Celtic, Roman, Arabic, Greek, Egyptian, Persian, and even Hindu deities with Shrines and temples in the Roman period. Any map covering the populated areas of Europe would need to cover the wild complexity. Southeast Asia would be as complex. Given the complexity of US religion in our reality, in this world American Pantheon distribution would be beyond mapping.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-14T16:03:20-08:00","id":1528572,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map?p=1528511#post1528511\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Mediterranean basin would be complex. Rome spread the gods around. Britain alone had Celtic, Roman, Arabic, Greek, Egyptian, Persian, and even Hindu deities with Shrines and temples in the Roman period. Any map covering the populated areas of Europe would need to cover the wild complexity. Southeast Asia would be as complex. Given the complexity of US religion in our reality, in this world American Pantheon distribution would be beyond mapping.</div> </div> </div> </div>All this is complicated by the fact that arguably some non-Roman deities like Isis of the Netjer and Mithras of the Yazata arguably acquired Dei/Theoi mantles, given how popular they became among Roman citizens.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26114-muskrat","name":"Muskrat","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"75"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-15T05:45:33-08:00","id":1528588,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Manbat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map?p=1528484#post1528484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The \u00d5\u2019\u00c2mar\u00e3</span> means &quot;gods&quot; in one of the Tukano dialects while Ohoenrodite means &quot;mythological heroes&quot; in Kadiweu. I don't have a name for the Guarani pantheon right now, but that is certainly something I will look upon.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hey, passing by to do some due diligence. After I answered that the Ohoenrodite was the name of the Kadiweu pantheon I went to confirm what it meant, and I got negatively surprise to see I mistranslated a bit of French from a French -&gt; Kadiweu short dictionary, and Ohoenrodite means &quot;mythological hero&quot; not &quot;mythological heroes&quot;. Basically is another name to G\u00f4-no\u00eano-h\u00f4di, the main god of the Kadiweu.<br /> <br /> Finding a good replacement isn't so simple as the Kadiweu kind of only see G\u00f4-no\u00eano-h\u00f4di as a god (mostly as the same god as the Christian god), so there isn`t a term for the pantheon or group of gods. Took me a bit but, using a Kadiweu/Portuguese dictionary (<a href=\"https://www.sil.org/system/files/reapdata/74/06/08/74060839706011162756896570533590209458/KDDict.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">link, if anyone is interested</a>) I found two good replacements: Goniotagododi, which means &quot;our lords&quot;, and Niwicidi which means &quot;spirits&quot;. As the singular version of Goniotagododi is also used for the Christian god or Jesus Christ (and G\u00f4-no\u00eano-h\u00f4di is often placed as the Christian god) I am more inclined to consider Goniotagododi as a better replacement.<br /> <br /> Just informing you all here because I had, very recently, vouched for the wrong name, so I better correct myself.<br /> <br /> (Also, it is supposed to be Onoenr\u0303odite, not Ohoenrodite, so a failure all around. I will triple-check names for my next projects.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12934,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","user":"Manbat","date":".  Adding the proper writting of Ohoenrodite 01-15-2024, 08:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","name":"Manbat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12934_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-15T10:07:16-08:00","id":1528597,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Manbat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map?p=1528588#post1528588\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hey, passing by to do some due diligence. After I answered that the Ohoenrodite was the name of the Kadiweu pantheon I went to confirm what it meant, and I got negatively surprise to see I mistranslated a bit of French from a French -&gt; Kadiweu short dictionary, and Ohoenrodite means &quot;mythological hero&quot; not &quot;mythological heroes&quot;. Basically is another name to G\u00f4-no\u00eano-h\u00f4di, the main god of the Kadiweu.<br /> <br /> Finding a good replacement isn't so simple as the Kadiweu kind of only see G\u00f4-no\u00eano-h\u00f4di as a god (mostly as the same god as the Christian god), so there isn`t a term for the pantheon or group of gods. Took me a bit but, using a Kadiweu/Portuguese dictionary (<a href=\"https://www.sil.org/system/files/reapdata/74/06/08/74060839706011162756896570533590209458/KDDict.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">link, if anyone is interested</a>) I found two good replacements: Goniotagododi, which means &quot;our lords&quot;, and Niwicidi which means &quot;spirits&quot;. As the singular version of Goniotagododi is also used for the Christian god or Jesus Christ (and G\u00f4-no\u00eano-h\u00f4di is often placed as the Christian god) I am more inclined to consider Goniotagododi as a better replacement.<br /> <br /> Just informing you all here because I had, very recently, vouched for the wrong name, so I better correct myself.<br /> <br /> (Also, it is supposed to be Onoenr\u0303odite, not Ohoenrodite, so a failure all around. I will triple-check names for my next projects.)</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm glad, although it seems you'll have to update the supplement xd<br /> And regarding the below, I would not consider it a mistake, sometimes to deal with traditional names they tend to be simplified<br /> There's a reason people call Nana'b'oozoo &quot;Nanabozho&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28200-plebsparks","name":"PlebSparks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28200_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-16T09:58:48-08:00","id":1528653,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PlebSparks</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map?p=1528597#post1528597\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm glad, although it seems you'll have to update the supplement xd<br /> And regarding the below, I would not consider it a mistake, sometimes to deal with traditional names they tend to be simplified<br /> There's a reason people call Nana'b'oozoo &quot;Nanabozho&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>I know I will have and I just did. The file and text on the supplement have been changed, and hopefully, in a few minutes, the DrivethruRPG page will be updated as well. I hope it is not a problem, or I will have to repost it, and that can be a problem if people buy it twice because of the name change.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12934-manbat","name":"Manbat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12934_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-21T14:09:35-08:00","id":1529105,"title":"","content":"What is the rule about plurals in pantheon names?<br /> In fan content, the Finnish pantheon is often referred to as &quot;Jumala&quot; instead of &quot;Jumalad&quot;, and at the same time the Korean pantheon is referred to as &quot;Sinduel&quot; instead of &quot;Sin&quot;. Also, I don't know Japanese, so I'm willing to be corrected. Why is it Kami and not kamigami?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28200-plebsparks","name":"PlebSparks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28200_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"22"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-28T10:15:28-08:00","id":1529603,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PlebSparks</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1383948-pantheon-map?p=1529105#post1529105\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What is the rule about plurals in pantheon names?<br /> In fan content, the Finnish pantheon is often referred to as &quot;Jumala&quot; instead of &quot;Jumalad&quot;, and at the same time the Korean pantheon is referred to as &quot;Sinduel&quot; instead of &quot;Sin&quot;. Also, I don't know Japanese, so I'm willing to be corrected. Why is it Kami and not kamigami?</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't know much about the Finnish gods, but iirc both &quot;Sin&quot; and &quot;Kami&quot; can be used as both singular and plural depending on the context, with &quot;Sinduel&quot; and &quot;Kamigami&quot; used when emphasizing the plural aspect. The same also applied to the Sh\u00e9n, considering both the Chinese and Korean words for &quot;god/gods&quot; originated from the Chinese word &quot;\u795e&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27963-jayng2183","name":"JayNg2183","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27963_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}